// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The Hyde Park walk. Sixteen stops told in the order the history
// happened, from Paul Cornell's stone at 53rd Street through the
// parks, the fair, the university, and the machinery of exclusion,
// ending back at 53rd and Harper. Thirteen stops are on the main
// line; three are optional detours, two southwest into Woodlawn off
// stop eleven and one northwest to Drexel Boulevard off stop
// fourteen, all drawn green and dashed on the map. Chronological order costs some geography, so the walk
// crosses its own path twice and says so on stop one. The narration
// is written from the founder's family oral history (a recorded
// conversation outlining the tour), corrected and deepened against
// the sources listed on every stop; nothing in the transcript should
// outrun them. Key print source for the 2026 expansion: Susan
// O'Connor Davis, Chicago's Historic Hyde Park (University of
// Chicago Press, 2013), plus the documentary series The Shame of
// Chicago, Shame of the Nation. The seven red plates are page-only
// and are not read into the audio; they run in teaching order across
// the walk, from the improvement clubs to the wrecking ball, and two
// of them (blockbusting and contract buying) sit on the Drexel
// Boulevard detour at the owner's direction, so the main-line prose
// at stop fourteen has to carry those mechanisms on its own. Audio
// is generated by scripts/walk-tts.mjs --tour hyde-park (OpenAI,
// voice ash); durations.json is the source of audioSeconds.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

